WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.000
Identity and privilege are central to system hardening.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:08.020
A system can have strong patching and logging but still be risky if too many users have

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.080
local administrator rights or if service accounts have broad permissions.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.380
Least privilege means users, administrators, services,

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.480
and applications receive the access they need for their role, not broad access by default.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.660
Standard user access should be the normal starting point.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.360
Administrative access should be rare, justified, monitored, and reviewed.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.900
Local administrator groups, privileged domain groups, cloud roles,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.520
endpoint management roles, and remote administration permissions should have clear owners.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.740
Shared administrator accounts should be avoided where possible because they weaken

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.000
accountability.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.940
Where shared or break-glass access is unavoidable,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.040
it needs stronger controls and careful logging.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.580
Service accounts need the same discipline.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.320
Each service account should have an owner, purpose, scope,

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:00.060
credential rotation expectation, and review schedule.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.320
A forgotten service account can retain powerful access long after the application changes.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.580
Do not treat service accounts as invisible plumbing.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.480
They are identities with permissions, and their risk should be understood.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.180
Emergency access is part of resilience.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.680
During an outage,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.300
administrators may need protected ways to recover systems even when normal identity

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.120
services are impaired.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.260
Those procedures should be documented, tested, and restricted.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.520
Good privilege management balances protection and recovery:

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.700
administrative power is limited and observable,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.760
but authorized teams can still restore service safely when something breaks.
